
Six kernel notification sources are registered by design. The sensor registers Process (PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutineEx), Thread (PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine), Image (PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutine), Object (ObRegisterCallbacks), Registry (CmRegisterCallbackEx), and File (FltRegisterFilter) callbacks. The article calls this "the entire 'where detection happens' surface."

{quote}csagent.sys registers six kernel notification sources. This is the entire 'where detection happens' surface.{quote}

Detection events are constructed from kernel memory, not ETW. CS_BuildCreationEvent builds the full process-creation record directly from internal kernel tracking structures — PID, parent PID, image path, command line, signer, reputation, SID, integrity.

{quote}CS_BuildCreationEvent is the sensor's full process-creation telemetry record: identity (PID/parent/name), provenance (image path, command line, TxF flag), trust (signer, reputation, SID/integrity) — everything the cloud rules need.{quote}

The detection dispatch consumes kernel event objects. The CS_DetectionDispatch function reads the kernel callback event object. There is no ETW input on this code path.

Evidence — ETW Is an Output Channel, Not a Detection Input:

The sensor writes ETW output, it does not read its own provider. The article lists ETW as one of the sensor's output mechanisms, alongside cloud upload and local logging.

{quote}Plus ETW output: EtwRegister / EtwWriteTransfer telemetry to CSFalconService.exe.{quote}

The spoofed provider's endpoint is user-mode, not the cloud. ETW output terminates at CSFalconService.exe, which writes to the Windows Event Log. It does not feed the kernel-sourced cloud pipeline.

